   173	### 1.1 Lemma RES and the coefficient floor [proved]
   174	
   175	**Lemma RES.** Let f, g ∈ Z[x] with deg f = d ≥ 1 and deg g = δ ≥ 1. Suppose they are coprime in Q[x] and
   176	f(m) ≡ g(m) ≡ 0 (mod N) for some integer m. Then N | Res(f, g), Res(f, g) ≠ 0, and
   177	N ≤ |Res(f, g)| ≤ ‖f‖₂^δ‖g‖₂^d.
   178	
   179	*Proof.*
   180	- The adjugate of the Sylvester matrix gives A, B ∈ Z[x] with Af + Bg = Res(f, g) (Lang, *Algebra*, IV §8).
   181	  Evaluating at m gives Res ≡ 0 (mod N).
   182	- Res ≠ 0 because f and g have no common root in Q̄.
   183	- The Sylvester matrix consists of δ shifted copies of f's coefficient vector and d copies of g's. Hadamard's
   184	  inequality on its rows gives |Res| ≤ ‖f‖₂^δ‖g‖₂^d. ∎
   185	
   186	**Corollary (linear side).** Let g = g₁x − g₀ and let f be irreducible of degree d ≥ 2. Then ‖f‖₂‖g‖₂^d ≥ N. Write
   187	a := log‖f‖₂ ≥ 0 and b := log‖g‖₂, so a + db ≥ t. Then a + b ≥ t/d + a(1 − 1/d) ≥ t/d. So
   188	**θ_coeff := (log‖f‖₂ + log‖g‖₂)/t ≥ 1/d** for every NFS pair with a linear side.
   189	- SNFS attains equality up to O(d log r/t).
   190	- RES also covers non-monic f, projective roots (via homogeneous resultants) and several rational sides pairwise
   191	  (codex snfsrig_2).
   192	- Skewed boxes change the weighted heights but not the resultant obstruction.
   193	
   194	`rigidity.check_res_floor` checks the divisibility and the Hadamard bound exactly on SNFS pairs.
   195	
   196	### 1.2 One level [proved (model arithmetic)]
   197	
   198	**Architecture.** The project's Dixon split + one-level special-q descent ([rec] §3.3). The premises are:
   199	- (R1) Split. The halves u₀, v₀ are products of k primes of size N^{1/(2k)}, with k = O(D^α).
   200	- (R2) Smoothness. θ = O(D^{2α−1}), in the random-integer model.
   201	- (R3) Norm product. With box radius V ≈ √ℓ·S, the rational value is ≈ ‖g‖V/ℓ and the algebraic value is ≈ ‖f‖V^d.
   202	  So θ = θ_coeff + (d − 1)ν with ν = 1/(4k), up to O(d log S/t).
   203	
   204	For SNFS, θ_SNFS = 1/d + (d − 1)/(4k). Theorem U's randomisation floors (h ≥ 2ν, level, window) are absent because
   205	nothing is randomised.
   206	
   207	**Prop UF.** Suppose θ_coeff ≥ c·d^{−p} with c > 0 and p ≥ 0. Then α ≥ (1 + p)/(3p + 2), and α ≥ 1/2 when p = 0.
   208	By the Corollary of RES the hypothesis holds with p = 1 for every pair with a linear side. So **α ≥ 2/5 for every
   209	one-level design of this architecture, special or not.**
   210	
   211	*Proof.*
   212	- Put d ≍ D^x and k ≍ D^y.
   213	- Applying (R2) to the two terms of θ gives px ≥ 1 − 2α and y − x ≥ 1 − 2α. (R1) gives y ≤ α.
   214	- Eliminating x and y: (1 − 2α)(1 + p)/p ≤ α, i.e. α ≥ (1 + p)/(3p + 2), which is decreasing in p.
   215	- See `sizemodel.one_level_floor`; the tests include a brute-force rational LP. ∎
   216	
   217	The scope is the stated architecture. "One level" means Dixon-split primes descended in one special-q step.
   218	Ordinary SNFS relation collection, with no split, is a different architecture (codex snfsrig_2).
   219	
   220	**Constants at α = 2/5.** Put d = d₀D^{1/5} and k = κD^{2/5}, and θ₀ := lim θD^{1/5} (`sizemodel.theta0`).

