   300	(§2.1) shows that polynomials *sharing the special root* either are multiples of f₀ or have height ≳ N^{1/d}. So in the
   301	model, a sub-2/5 rational-only tree must contain nodes whose pairs violate (M1).
   302	- For N ∈ 𝔈 the only such pairs we know are the special ones (and the generator changes of §2.4).
   303	- That *every* (M1)-violating pair with a known root is of this kind is **not proved**. It is the (M1) counting
   304	  heuristic. My draft's exhaustive classification is withdrawn.
   305	- Hybrid trees are not excluded.
   306	- Codex's blind round reached the same 1/3 ("the former forbidden window disappears").
   307	
   308	### 1.5 Recognition: Prop REC [proved accounting; unconditional]
   309	
   310	Each accepted row of the Dixon-split architecture requires the complete factorisation (the shape certificate) of
   311	the Gauss-reduced halves u₀, v₀ of a random square.
   312	- These are arbitrary integers ≤ √(2N) whose prime factors lie in the target set ⊂ (Y, 2Y], Y = N^{1/(2k)} =
   313	  L_N[1 − α].
   314	- Every intermediate node of a multi-level tree likewise has to factor rational cofactors with prime factors up to
   315	  L[1 − α].
   316	- These integers are not special, so the special-family algorithm cannot be called on them. That would be circular
   317	  (codex snfsrig_1 §1, "algorithmic qualification").
   318	
   319	**Prop REC.** A recogniser with a proved expected cost L_p[β, O(1)] per prime p found, for all p in a target set of
   320	density (log N)^{−O(1)}, meets the budget L_N[α]^{O(1)} iff β(1 − α) ≤ α, i.e. **α ≥ β/(1 + β)**. The bounds
   321	available:
   322	
   323	| recogniser | proved bound | α threshold |
   324	|---|---|---|
   325	| Theorem U recursion on u₀, v₀ (internal) | L_{√(2N)}[2/5, c_U] = L_N[2/5, 2^{−2/5}c_U] | 2/5 |
   326	| HECM (Lenstra–Pila–Pomerance) | L_p[2/3, c₀] (part III unpublished, §5) | 2/5 (lemmaM (c)) |
   327	| averaged ECM (Pomerance 1987; explicit form Lenstra–Pomerance 1992 §7) | exp(O((log y)^{6/7})), recognisable primes only | 6/13 |
   328	| trial division, Pollard–Strassen, batch smoothness | Y^{Ω(1)}, or total cost ≥ π(Y) | 1 − α ≤ α only for α ≥ 1/2 |
   329	| **under RH:** genus-2 HECM [g2rh] (RH + Younis, unrefereed) | L_p[1/2, 2 + o(1)] for every p | **1/3** |
   330	
   331	So, **unconditionally**, none of these guarantees establishes recognition within L[α] for α < 2/5. These are the
   332	proved upper bounds of the known methods; they are not lower bounds for recognition (codex snfsrig_2).
   333	
   334	**Target restriction does not help cheaply.** Lemma S″ accepts any target set G ⊂ J. But with a set of relative
   335	density δ, the counting guarantee drops by ≈ δ^{2k} with k ≍ D^α, so the guarantee needs δ ≥ (log N)^{−O(1)}. For
   336	example, the primes in J with p − 1 being L[α]-smooth number at most Ψ(2Y, B). That is a relative density exp(−(1 +
   337	o(1))u log u) with u ≍ D^{1−2α}, so δ^{2k} = exp(−Θ(D^{1−α}log D)) is not L[α]^{O(1)}. This is an upper bound for the
   338	guarantee, not a proof that other target sets fail.
   339	
   340	*Proof of the table.* The first row uses L_{√x}[α, c] = L_x[α, 2^{−α}c + o(1)] ([rec] §3.1). The other rows insert
   341	y = 2Y = L_N[1 − α] into the cited bounds. ∎
   342	
   343	---
