   345	## 2. Recovering randomness: what survives
   346	
   347	The relation-supply proofs use field randomness in four places ([U] §§4–6, [ld], thmA §1) [assessment from reading
   348	those proofs]:
   349	- **(R) Theorem R / FIB.** For fixed P, G_g(P) = g₁a − g₀b is near-uniform on an interval as g ranges over the 2D box
   350	  𝒢 = [M/2, M]². Each value n has mass |𝒢_P(n)|/|𝒢| ≤ 16/(MV). This gives rational smoothness.
   351	- **(GM) GM-U, one-point smoothing.** For fixed P, F_A(P) = F̂(P) + G(P)⟨c, v(P)⟩ ranges over an AP mod G(P) as c ranges
   352	  over [−H, H]^d. This gives algebraic smoothness via Lemma 8.5′.
   353	- **(K) Kern-U.** For P ≠ P′, the pair (F_A(P), F_A(P′)) is near-uniform over c (a dimension-free fibre count). This is
   354	  LD*'s Step 1.
   355	- **(W) Lemma W.** F̂(g₀, g₁) = Nw with w a random prime spreads the forced residue F_A(P) ≡ wN(b/g₁)^d mod G(P). This
   356	  is LD*'s Step 4.
   357	
   358	### 2.1 Lemma HR (height rigidity at the special root) [proved]
   359	
   360	Let f₀ ∈ Z[x] be irreducible of degree d with f₀(m) ≡ 0 (mod N). Let f ∈ Z[x] have degree δ ≥ 1 with f(m) ≡ 0
   361	(mod N), and suppose f₀ ∤ f in Q[x]. Then ‖f‖₂ ≥ (N/‖f₀‖₂^δ)^{1/d}.
   362	
   363	*Proof.* f₀ is irreducible and does not divide f, so the two are coprime. Apply Lemma RES. ∎
   364	
   365	**Consequences for N ∈ 𝔈 with f₀ = f_d and δ ≤ d.** We get ‖f‖₂ ≥ N^{1/d}/(√2·r^d|s|). This is N^{(1−o(1))/d} when
   366	d² log r + d log|s| = o(t). So any polynomial with the root m_d that is not a multiple of f_d, including any other
   367	defining polynomial of K_d, has height ≥ N^{(1−o(1))/d}. Its algebraic values then have *typical* size ≥ N^{1/d}V^d.
   368	
   369	**Scope (codex).** This is uniqueness at a fixed known root and bounded degree. The other special degrees use other
   370	roots m_{d′}; they are available, and they are among the polynomially many pairs covered by Prop RB.
   371	
   372	**Exhaustive check.** `rigidity.exhaustive_small_height` enumerates all f of degree ≤ 3 and height ≤ 3 with f(m) ≡ 0.
   373	- For N = 2^{30} − 3 it finds only ±f₀.
   374	- For N = 509, where HR guarantees nothing, non-multiples appear, all consistent with the bound.
   375	
   376	### 2.2 Lemma PH (perturbation height) [proved; codex snfsrig_1 §2]
   377	
   378	For m > 1 and 0 ≠ c ∈ Z[x], ‖(x − m)c‖_∞ ≥ (m − 1)‖c‖_∞. Hence ‖f₀ + (x − m)c‖_∞ ≥ (m − 1)‖c‖_∞ − ‖f₀‖_∞.
   379	
   380	*Proof.* Pick i with |c_i| = ‖c‖_∞. The x^i-coefficient of (x − m)c is c_{i−1} − mc_i (with c_{−1} := 0), so its
   381	absolute value is ≥ m|c_i| − |c_{i−1}| ≥ (m − 1)‖c‖_∞. ∎
   382	
   383	So GM/Kern-type perturbations of the special field cost height ≈ H·N^{1/d} at once. The resulting pair satisfies (M1):
   384	(d + 1)t/d + 2t/d ≥ t.
   385	
   386	### 2.3 Lemma RS (rational-side rigidity) [proved]
   387	
   388	Let 0 ≤ m < N and M ≥ 1 with (m + 1)M < N. Let (g₀, g₁) ∈ Z² satisfy g₀ ≡ mg₁ (mod N) and |g₀|, |g₁| ≤ M. Then
   389	g₀ = mg₁. So the only primitive pairs are ±(m, 1), and G_g(P) = ±(a − bm).
   390	
   391	*Proof.* N divides mg₁ − g₀, and |mg₁ − g₀| ≤ (m + 1)M < N. ∎
   392	
   393	**Consequences.**
   394	- The 2D-random rational side (R) with the special root would need M ≥ N/(m + 1) ≈ N^{1−1/d}. That gives rational
   395	  values ≥ N^{1−1/d}V/ℓ and θ ≥ 1 − 1/d.
   396	- A random root m′ (BLP base-(m₁, m₂)) restores (R) but discards f₀; that is GNFS.
   397	- (My draft's remark on Cunningham roots mζ^i was wrong and is deleted. Those roots are powers of r and give the other
   398	  special fields, which RB covers; both reviewers.)
   399	
   400	### 2.4 Prop DI (device invariance for the listed devices) [proved]
   401	
   402	Let F₀(a, b) := b^d f₀(a/b) and G₀(a, b) := a − mb. For each device below, the pair (F, G) satisfies
   403	(F, G)(P) = (c₁F₀(TP), c₂G₀(TP)) for all P ∈ Z², with T ∈ M₂(Z), det T ≠ 0, and c_i ∈ Q^×. The field is Q(α₀)
   404	via the corresponding Möbius image of α₀. Compatibility with ψ needs the determinants and denominators to be units
   405	mod N; a gcd check handles them.
   406	
   407	| device | T | (c₁, c₂) |
   408	|---|---|---|
   409	| multiplier N·w, kN (f = wf₀, same root) | id | (w, 1) |
   410	| root twist / scaling by u (root um, f = u^d f₀(x/u)) | diag(1, u) | (1, 1) |
   411	| shift f₀(x + t) (root m − t) | [[1, t], [0, 1]] | (1, 1) |
   412	| Möbius (γx + δ)^d f₀((αx + β)/(γx + δ)), G := G₀∘T | [[α, β], [γ, δ]] | (1, 1) |
   413	| same d, exponent j + dh: r^{j+dh}N = (r^h m)^d − s r^{j+dh} | diag(1, r^h) | (1, 1) |
   414	
   415	*Proof.* Each identity is the substitution x = TP in the homogenised forms. The table was corrected after codex
   416	snfsrig_2 and the Opus critic: the last row has T = diag(1, r^h), c₁ = 1, and only j′ ≡ j (mod d) with j′ ≥ j. Other
   417	residues of j give different fields. `rigidity.device_identities` checks the first four rows symbolically. ∎
   418	
   419	**Consequences.**
   420	- On a box Ω, a device's values are those of the fixed pair (F₀, G₀) on TΩ (scaled). A non-unimodular T changes the
   421	  sampled sublattice, and a shift changes the region.
   422	- For a prime w > B every nonzero value of wF₀ is non-smooth. After removing the content nothing changes (codex).
   423	  Hence N·w gives no new randomness, and Lemma W has nothing to act on.
   424	- **Not exhaustive.** A generator change such as x⁵ − s ↦ x⁵ − s² (root m², α ↦ α²) keeps the field but is *not*
   425	  Möbius. Its degree-one values are degree-two elements in α₀ (codex snfsrig_2). Such changes, and all other
   426	  polynomially many special pairs, are covered by Prop RB, not by DI.
