    76	  2010 (constant 3/√2) and in clean form since 2024 (constant 2). It merits at most a short note
    77	  or remark.
    78	  - The genuinely unpublished piece is the algorithmic accounting (LPP III), which is routine.
    79	  - The L_N corollary is the only part that "beats L_N[1/2,1]". It does so only for inputs with
    80	    a factor below N^{1/4}, and only under RH.
    81	
    82	**V4 (side result; sketch, bookkeeping as in §4 but not written out).** Use Prop A and Prop B
    83	with LPP I Thm 1.3 (unconditional; §6.3) in place of Younis. This gives an unconditional expected
    84	time of L_p[2/3, 2^{2/3}c₁ + o(1)] (log N)^{5+o(1)}, with c₁ = 1,980,000^{1/3}.
    85	- That is a weaker form of LPP I's Thm 1.1, which claims (log n)², and whose algorithmic half
    86	  depended on the unwritten part III.
    87	- Our polylog is worse because the separation step at q costs λ(q) (§5).
    88	
    89	**V5 (optimisation inside the analysis model: cost = s + g/(2s); not a lower bound for all
    90	methods).**
    91	- s = 1 is optimal (cost s + 1/s).
    92	- Genus g ≥ 3 is worse: the cost is √(2g) even if LPP-type fibre bounds existed, and such
    93	  bounds are not known.
    94	- Genus 1 would give √2. It needs θ = 1/2, which Younis-type RH methods cannot reach.
    95	- Split Jacobians, stage-2 continuations and large-prime variants change only L^{o(1)} factors.
    96	- So, within this cost model, 2 is the best this route gives (§6).
    97	
    98	**V6 (unconditional / almost-all; not available).** No unconditional theorem known to us gives
    99	L[1/2]-smooth density in the LPP interval, even for almost all p.
   100	- Matomäki–Radziwiłł need fixed u.
   101	- Matomäki 2016 (IJNT), Younis Thm 1.1 and Jain (Quart. J. Math. 2026, arXiv:2502.10530) all
   102	  need y ≥ exp(C(log x)^{2/3}(log log x)^{4/3}) or more.
   103	- Hildebrand–Tenenbaum Thm 5.6 needs y ≥ exp((log X)^{5/6+ε}).
   104	- Under RH, Hafner's unpublished almost-all result reaches y ≥ L(X). See §7.
   105	
   106	## 1. Notation
   107	
   108	- L_x[a, c] = exp(c (log x)^a (log log x)^{1-a}).
   109	- P^+(n) is the largest prime factor of n, and Ψ(x, y) = #{n ≤ x : P^+(n) ≤ y}.
   110	- I_p = [p² − ½p^{3/2}, p² + ½p^{3/2}].
   111	- λ(t) = (log t)²(log log t)².
   112	- A tuple is τ = (a, b, f_1, …, f_5) ∈ (Z/N)^7. It defines f_0 := b² − Σ_{i≥1} f_i a^i,
   113	  f = Σ f_i X^i, and the point (a, b) on y² = f(x).
   114	
   115	## 2. The two analytic inputs: exact statements
   116	
   117	**LPP II = H. W. Lenstra Jr., J. Pila, C. Pomerance, "A hyperelliptic smoothness test, II",
   118	Proc. London Math. Soc. (3) 84 (2002) 105–146, Theorem 1.1** (verbatim modulo typesetting; from
   119	math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper129.pdf, checked against people.maths.ox.ac.uk/pila/HyperII.pdf):
   120	
   121	> Let p be a prime number with p > 8100, and let F_p denote the prime field of cardinality p.
   122	> Then for all but at most p integers z in the interval [p² − ½p^{3/2}, p² + ½p^{3/2}] there
   123	> are at least p^{9/2} / (24,000 · (log p)² · (log log p)²) quintic polynomials f ∈ F_p[X] with
   124	> non-vanishing discriminants such that #J_f(F_p) = z.
   125	
   126	- Quantifiers: fixed p > 8100; the exceptional set has size ≤ p; the bound holds for every
   127	  non-exceptional z.
   128	- "Quintic" means degree exactly 5, not necessarily monic. There are ~p^6 of them, so the
   129	  average fibre is ~p^{9/2}/8 over the full Weil interval.
   130	- J_f is the Jacobian of the smooth genus-2 curve y² = f(x).
   131	- LPP II states only lower bounds. They write that an upper bound "of the same form" is
   132	  believed but not proved. We need no upper bound.
   133	
   134	**Younis = K. Younis, "Asymptotics for smooth numbers in short intervals", arXiv:2409.05761v1
   135	(9 Sep 2024; no later version, no journal reference as of 2026-10-18), Theorem 1.3:**
   136	
   137	> Assume the Riemann Hypothesis is true. Let 1/2 < θ ≤ 1. There exists a constant K = K(θ) > 2,
   138	> as well as a function f_θ(x, y) which is independent of h, such that the estimates
   139	>   (Ψ(x+h, y) − Ψ(x, y))/h = α Ψ(x, y)/x · (1 + O_θ(min{1/u + h log(u+1)/(x log y), log(u+1)/log y}))
   140	> and [a second estimate with main term f_θ] hold uniformly for x^θ ≤ h ≤ x and (log x)^K ≤ y ≤ 2x.
   141	
   142	- Here u = log x / log y, and α = α(x, y) is the saddle point.
   143	- By Hildebrand–Tenenbaum 1986 (Lemma 2; Younis (1.5)), α = 1 − (log(u log(u+1)) + O(1))/log y
   144	  for log x < y ≤ x. In our range this gives α → 1.
   145	- **Primary input: Thm 1.3 itself, with α → 1.**
   146	- Within the RH proof, §7.1 also derives an α-free form, (7.2):
   147	  (Ψ(x+h,y) − Ψ(x,y))/h = Ψ(x,y)/x · (1 + O(log(u+1)/log y)).
   148	  - It is valid for (log x)^K ≤ y ≤ x^{1/2−ε} and θ ≥ 1/2 + 10ε.
   149	  - The additional condition y ≥ exp((log log x)^{5/3+1/100}) enters only through Hildebrand's
   150	    long-interval (h = x) step.
   151	  - Our parameters satisfy all of these conditions.
   152	- Quantifiers: θ is fixed, and the implied constants depend on θ only. We take θ = 3/4.
   153	
   154	**CEP** (Canfield–Erdős–Pomerance, J. Number Theory 17 (1983), Cor. to Thm 3.1): uniformly for
   155	u → ∞ with y ≥ (log x)^{1+ε}, Ψ(x, y) = x · u^{−u(1+o(1))}. The o(1) is O(log log u / log u).
   156	
   157	**Weil.** Let C/F_r be a genus-2 curve with a squarefree quintic model y² = F(X), where r is odd.
   158	Then #C(F_r) = r + 1 − a with |a| ≤ 4√r, and #J(F_r) ∈ [(√r − 1)^4, (√r + 1)^4]. The model
   159	has one point at infinity, so the number of affine points is A(F) = #C(F_r) − 1 ∈ [r − 4√r, r + 4√r].
   160	
   161	## 3. The algorithm and its algorithmic accounting
   162	
   163	### 3.1 Algorithm C (one curve; operational, src/genus2rh/hecm.py:one_curve)
   164	
   165	Input: odd N with no prime factor ≤ 8100; parameters v (a guess for p) and w (smoothness bound).
   166	
   167	1. Draw τ uniformly from (Z/N)^7. Set f_0 := b² − Σ_{i=1}^5 f_i a^i, so that f(a) = b².
   168	2. Let g = gcd(f_5, N). If 1 < g < N, output g. If g = N, stop with no output.
   169	3. Pass to the monic model F(X) = f_5^4 f(X/f_5), with point (A, B) = (f_5 a, f_5² b).
   170	   - This is an isomorphism over Z/N because f_5 is a unit.
   171	   - disc F = f_5^{12} disc f.
   172	4. Let g = gcd(disc F mod N, N). If 1 < g < N, output g. If g = N, stop.
   173	5. Set D := (X − A, B), a Mumford pair: u = X − A is monic and u | F − B².
   174	   - Let e(r) := max{m : r^m ≤ (⌊√v⌋ + 2)^4}.
   175	   - For each prime r ≤ w in increasing order, repeat e(r) times: D ← [r]D. This uses
   176	     double-and-add, and each group operation is Cantor's composition-and-reduction over Z/N.
   177	   - Every polynomial is normalised. Coefficients ≡ 0 mod N are stripped, and the top
   178	     coefficient c is tested.
   179	   - If 1 < gcd(c, N) < N, output gcd(c, N). This is the only way a "NonUnit" arises.
   180	6. Otherwise stop with no output.
   181	
   182	**Lemma 1 (sampling; proved).**
   183	- (a) For each prime r | N, τ mod r is uniform on F_r^7.
   184	- (b) For distinct primes p, q | N, τ mod p and τ mod q are independent. Reason:
   185	  Z/N → Z/pq ≅ F_p × F_q is a surjective ring map with fibres of equal size.
   186	- (c) Fix a squarefree quintic f over F_r. Exactly A(f) tuples mod r have f_1, …, f_5 equal
   187	  to the coefficients of f and f(a) = b². Their points are exactly the affine points of
   188	  y² = f(x). So Pr[τ mod r gives f] = A(f)/r^7 ≥ (1 − 4r^{−1/2}) r^{−6}.
   189	
   190	**Lemma 2 (outputs are proper divisors; proved).**
   191	- Each output is gcd(c, N) with c ≢ 0 mod N and gcd(c, N) ≠ 1, so it is a proper divisor.
   192	- Steps 2 and 4 output only when 1 < g < N.
   193	
   194	**Lemma 3 (reduction lemma for Cantor over Z/N; proved).** Suppose a run passes steps 2 and 4
   195	with gcd = 1 and enters step 5. Suppose also that step 5, or a prefix of it, ends without a
   196	NonUnit. Then for every prime r | N:
   197	- (i) the reduction mod r of every intermediate value equals the corresponding value of the
   198	  same program run over F_r;
   199	- (ii) every intermediate polynomial has the same degree over Z/N and over F_r.
   200	
   201	Hence the computed pair reduces mod r to the correct Mumford representative of the
   202	corresponding element of J_F(F_r).
   203	
   204	*Proof.* The program is a sequence of ring operations, divisions with remainder by
   205	polynomials whose top coefficient was checked to be a unit, inversions of such units, and
   206	branches. Every branch (Euclid's "while r ≠ 0", Cantor's "while deg u > 2", the bits of r)
   207	depends only on degrees and zero tests. The top coefficient of a normalised polynomial is a
   208	unit mod N, hence nonzero mod r, so degrees agree. A coefficient ≡ 0 mod N is ≡ 0 mod r. Ring
   209	operations, unit inversion, and division with remainder by a polynomial with unit leading
   210	coefficient all commute with reduction mod r. Quotient and remainder are unique, and over Z/N
   211	the quotient is just retained. Induction on the steps proves (i) and (ii).
   212	- Over F_r, F is a squarefree monic quintic: F_5 = 1 and disc F is a unit by step 4. So
   213	  Cantor's algorithm computes the group law of J_F(F_r) with identity (1, 0) (Cantor, Math.
   214	  Comp. 48 (1987); Koblitz, J. Cryptology 1 (1989)).
   215	- Over a field, no NonUnit can arise.
   216	- Experimental check: G1b, 200 runs at 30-bit p, q, 0 mismatches (§8). ∎
   217	
   218	**Proposition A (separation; genus-2 Lenstra 1987 Prop. (2.6); proved).** Let p ≠ q be primes
   219	dividing N, and let τ satisfy:
   220	- (i) p ≤ v;
   221	- (ii) f_5 ≢ 0 and disc f ≢ 0 mod p;
   222	- (iii) #J_f(F_p) is w-smooth;
   223	- (iv) f_5 ≢ 0 and disc f ≢ 0 mod q;
   224	- (v) ℓ ∤ #J_f(F_q), where ℓ := P^+(ord D_p) and D_p := D mod p ∈ J(F_p).
   225	
   226	Then Algorithm C on τ outputs a proper divisor of N.
   227	
   228	*Proof.*
   229	1. By (ii) and (iv), the gcds in steps 2 and 4 are ≠ N. If either is ≠ 1, it is output.
   230	   Otherwise step 5 runs.
   231	2. D_p = [P − ∞] with P = (A, B) affine. It is nonzero, since a genus ≥ 1 curve has no
   232	   degree-1 function, and deg u = 1. So ω := ord D_p > 1.
   233	3. ω divides #J(F_p), which is w-smooth, so ℓ ≤ w.
   234	4. For each prime r, r^{v_r(ω)} ≤ #J(F_p) ≤ (√p + 1)^4 < (⌊√v⌋ + 2)^4, so v_r(ω) ≤ e(r).
   235	5. Let m := v_ℓ(ω) ≥ 1 and k_0 := ∏_{r<ℓ} r^{e(r)} · ℓ^{m−1}. Then ω ∤ k_0 and ω | k_0 ℓ.
   236	   The schedule is increasing and repeats ℓ e(ℓ) ≥ m times, so it computes [k_0]D and then,
   237	   at the next step, [k_0 ℓ]D.
   238	6. Suppose no NonUnit arises up to and including [k_0 ℓ]D. By Lemma 3:
   239	   - [k_0 ℓ]D reduces mod p to [k_0 ℓ]D_p = 0. Its u-part has degree 0 mod p, hence over Z/N.
   240	     It is monic, so u = 1, and so [k_0 ℓ]D_q = 0 as well.

