   252	**Lemma 4 (supply at the second prime; proved from LPP II Thm 1.1).** Let q > 8100 be prime and
   253	ℓ any prime. Let V_ℓ(q) be the set of τ ∈ F_q^7 such that (iv) holds and ℓ ∤ #J_f(F_q). Then
   254	#V_ℓ(q) ≥ q^7 / (144,000 λ(q)).
   255	
   256	*Proof.*
   257	1. I_q contains ≥ ⌊q^{3/2}⌋ integers. At least (1 − 1/ℓ)q^{3/2} − 2 ≥ q^{3/2}/2 − 2 of them are
   258	   prime to ℓ.
   259	2. Removing LPP's ≤ q exceptions leaves ≥ q^{3/2}/3 values z, since q > 8100.
   260	3. Each such z has ≥ q^{9/2}/(24,000 λ(q)) quintics f with nonzero discriminant and
   261	   #J_f = z. These sets are disjoint for distinct z, which gives ≥ q^6/(72,000 λ(q))
   262	   quintics f.
   263	4. Each f comes from A(f) ≥ q − 4√q ≥ q/2 tuples (Lemma 1(c)). ∎
   264	
   265	**Proposition B (per-curve success probability; proved from Lemma 1, Prop A, LPP II).** Let p
   266	be the least prime factor of N, with p > 8100 and p ≤ v, and let q ≠ p be another prime
   267	factor. Put S_w(p) := #{z ∈ I_p ∩ Z : P^+(z) ≤ w}. Then
   268	
   269	  Pr[Algorithm C outputs a proper divisor] ≥ (S_w(p) − p) · (1 − 4p^{−1/2}) / (24,000 λ(p) p^{3/2}) · 1/(144,000 λ(q)).
   270	
   271	*Proof.*
   272	1. Let T_p be the set of residues t ∈ F_p^7 satisfying (ii) and (iii), and let
   273	   E := {τ : τ mod p ∈ T_p and τ mod q ∈ V_{ℓ(τ mod p)}(q)}. Here ℓ(t) = P^+(ord D_p) is a
   274	   function of t.
   275	2. By Prop A, success ⊇ E.
   276	3. By Lemma 1(b), Pr[E] = Σ_{t∈T_p} p^{−7} · #V_{ℓ(t)}(q) q^{−7}
   277	   ≥ (#T_p/p^7) · min_ℓ #V_ℓ(q)/q^7.
   278	4. Every non-exceptional w-smooth z ∈ I_p contributes ≥ p^{9/2}/(24,000 λ(p)) quintics. Each
   279	   quintic contributes A(f) ≥ p − 4√p tuples. So #T_p ≥ (S_w(p) − p) p^{9/2} (p − 4√p)/(24,000 λ(p)).
   280	5. Apply Lemma 4. ∎
   281	
   282	**Cost of Algorithm C (proved).**
   283	- Steps 1–4 take (log N)^{O(1)} bit operations. The discriminant is a 9×9 Sylvester
   284	  determinant, computed with integer Bareiss.
   285	- Step 5 performs ≤ Σ_{r≤w} e(r)(2⌈log₂ r⌉) ≤ 4π(w) log₂((⌊√v⌋+2)^4) + O(π(w)) group
   286	  operations, which is O(π(w) log v).
   287	- Each group operation is O(1) operations on polynomials of degree ≤ 10 over Z/N, plus O(1)
   288	  extended gcds of degree ≤ 4 and O(1) inversions: O((log N)²) bit operations classically.
   289	- Total per curve: O(w (log v)(log N)²/log w).
   290	- The prime list up to w is sieved once per stage in O(w log log w).
