codex/snfsrig_1.out:45:**Heuristic (size model).** Random-integer smoothness allows a value of logarithmic size \(O(T)\) to be \(B\)-smooth at cost \(\exp(O(E))\). Merely assuming \(H_f=N^{o(1)}\) is insufficient: one needs \(\log H_f=O(T)\), with suitable constants. The explicit SNFS heights above comfortably satisfy this at the degrees used below.
codex/snfsrig_1.out:89:At the last level, smoothness to \(B\) requires approximately
codex/snfsrig_1.out:125:**Proved—dependency distinction.** Kern is an algebraic fibre-counting lemma for a full coefficient box. GM is conditional smoothness at a good modulus, obtained by coefficient averaging. Theorem R needs the unrestricted two-dimensional rational-side distribution. Lemma W needs an independent multiplier with sufficiently spread residues. FM and LD* assemble these inputs. Changing coordinates does not automatically reproduce their probability laws.
codex/snfsrig_1.out:217:**Literature—Semaev’s special primes.** I. A. Semaev, “Special prime numbers and discrete logs in finite prime fields,” *Math. Comp.* **71** (2002), 363–377, DOI **10.1090/S0025-5718-00-01308-9**. It develops resultant constructions for special primes, including bounded-coefficient exponential forms. The stated \(L_p[1/3,(32/9)^{1/3}]\) expected runtime is explicitly **heuristic**. Its algebraic identities do not establish the needed smoothness distributions. [Article text](https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=15fa3b43f5c70796caa135ee088cffa43a2ef497&repid=rep1&type=pdf).
codex/snfsrig_1.out:223:**Literature—rigorous \(1/3\), with a crucial qualification.** J. D. Lee–R. Venkatesan, “Rigorous analysis of a randomised number field sieve,” *J. Number Theory* **187** (2018), 92–159, DOI **10.1016/j.jnt.2017.10.019**:
codex/snfsrig_1.out:226:- Theorem 2.3 obtains factoring for \(N=pq\), \(p\equiv q\equiv3\pmod4\), assuming their character-decorrelation Conjecture 7.1.
codex/snfsrig_1.out:262:**Literature—important exception.** R. Barbulescu–A. Lachand, “Some mathematical remarks on the polynomial selection in NFS,” *Math. Comp.* **86** (2017), 397–418, DOI **10.1090/mcom/3112**, Theorem 4.2, proves substantially stronger asymptotics for fixed imaginary quadratic forms of fundamental discriminant, reaching \(y\ge\exp((\log\log X)^{5/3+\varepsilon})\). Thus “all known binary-form results require power-type smoothness” would be false. The theorem does not provide growing-degree uniformity, special-\(q\) restrictions, or simultaneous smoothness with the large rational form. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1403.0184).
codex/snfsrig_1.out:266:**Open.** Small SNFS norms therefore remove a model obstruction without supplying the required distribution theorem. The decisive unresolved statement is uniform smoothness of the actual sparse, correlated fixed-form values—not simply smoothness of integers of the same size.
