+### 2.5 Prop RB (relativisation barrier for small fixed families) [proved, for a listed class of inputs]
+
+**Setting.**
+- A one-level design 𝔇 is given by Φ fixed pairs (f_i, g_i), with devices allowed, and special-q targets ℓ ∈ J.
+  Each target is searched in a box of ≤ W lattice points; [U] has W ≤ 400S².
+- For 𝒮 ⊂ Z, let γ_𝔇(𝒮) be the descendability density of [U] §1 computed with 𝒮 in place of the B-smooth integers
+  𝒮_B. The filter keeps the rational cofactor in [X_r/C, X_r].
+- Let 𝒱 be the set of all rational cofactors G_i(P)/ℓ over all (i, ℓ, P). Then |𝒱| ≤ Φπ_JW ≤ N^{(2+o(1))ν} whenever
+  ΦW = N^{o(ν)}.
+
+**Input class 𝒦.** The class consists of the following statements, applied to the integer set 𝒮 at sizes x ≥ X_r/C.
+- (ii) upper bounds #(𝒮 ∩ I) ≤ U(I): DS-U, L2_fam, Rankin, Brun–Titchmarsh-type.
+- (iii) two-sided estimates Σ_{I∈ℐ} w_I|#(𝒮 ∩ I) − main(I)| ≤ err, with weights |w_I| ≤ 1 and a *fixed* main(I).
+  Examples: HT local estimates, Lemma U1, Ψ in a single AP, and BV/BDH-type sums with Σ over moduli and max over
+  residues, as in Lemma 8.5′(R′).
+- Multiplicative closure of 𝒮 is **not** in 𝒦; deletion destroys it.
+
+**Prop RB.**
+- (i) γ_𝔇(𝒮_B ∖ 𝒱) = 0.
+- (ii) Every type-(ii) input holds for 𝒮′ := 𝒮_B ∖ 𝒱.
+- (iii) Every type-(iii) input holds for 𝒮′ with err replaced by err + |𝒱|·|ℐ|.
+
+Hence a proof of γ_𝔇 ≥ γ₀ > 0 from inputs in 𝒦, valid with some slack (each type-(iii) input holding with error
+err − |𝒱|·|ℐ|), would also prove γ_𝔇(𝒮′) ≥ γ₀. That contradicts (i). Such a proof therefore cannot exist.
 
 *Proof.* (i): every success requires some G_i(P)/ℓ ∈ 𝒮, and 𝒱 ∩ 𝒮′ = ∅. (ii): monotonicity. (iii): for each I,
 |#(𝒮_B ∩ I) − #(𝒮′ ∩ I)| ≤ |𝒱|. ∎
 
-**The SNFS numbers [proved arithmetic].**
-- *Size of 𝒱.* Φ = (log N)^{O(1)} (the fields K_d, d ≤ D, together with devices whose T have entries
-  (log N)^{O(1)}). Also π_J = N^{2ν+o(1)} and W = L^{O(1)}, so |𝒱| = N^{2ν+o(1)}.
-- *Size of the counted values.* The rational values have size X_r = N^{1/d−ν+o(1)}.
-- *Inputs on intervals or single APs.* These count sets I of integers of size x ≥ X_r with expected size
-  ≥ xρ/B^{O(1)} and budget ε·(main), ε = L^{−O(1)}. Their deletion error |𝒱| is negligible because 1/d − ν > 2ν.
-- *Barban–Davenport–Halberstam / Bombieri–Vinogradov-type sums* (Lemma 8.5′). These range over |ℐ| ≤ x^{1/2} classes,
-  with budget ≍ ε·Ψ(x). The deletion error is ≤ |𝒱|·x^{1/2}, which is ≤ ε·xρ iff |𝒱| ≤ ερx^{1/2}. At x ≥ X_r this holds
-  iff 2ν < (1/d − ν)/2, i.e. **5ν < 1/d (d < 4k/5)**.
-- *Regime.* This holds throughout the regime that matters: at the 2/5 design d ≍ D^{1/5} and ν ≍ D^{−2/5}, and for every
-  fixed d once k > 5d/4. Algebraic values are larger still.
-
-*Contrast with Theorem U [consistency check].* There the union over the field law of the values F_A(P) is an AP of
-length ≍ HV^{d−1}. The point masses are ≤ 1/((2H + 1)V′^{d−1}) ([U] Lemma SM1). Deleting K values changes FM by
-≤ K·#𝒫·max mass, which is negligible for any K = N^{O(ν)}. So the same statistical inputs do suffice. The barrier is
-the small support of a fixed family, exactly Prop SI(i)/(ii) of [nest] but for the plain smooth set.
-
-**What a proof would need instead.** It would need non-relativising, form-specific information: arithmetic of the
-specific values (Norm(a − bα₀) in a Kummer field, a − b·r^j in base r), such as type I/II sums for incomplete norm forms
-(Heath-Brown, Maynard), BBDT's methods for binary forms, or prescribed-digit methods (Bourgain). §4–§5 compare the
-required ranges with what these give.
+**SNFS numerology [proved arithmetic].**
+- *Sizes.* Φ = (log N)^{O(1)} covers the K_d, the generator changes and devices with entries (log N)^{O(1)}. With
+  π_J ≤ 2Y = O(N^{2ν}) and W = L^{O(1)}, we get |𝒱| ≤ N^{(2+o(1))ν}. The values have size x ≥ X_r/C = N^{1/d−ν−o(1)}.
+- *Single sets.* Single intervals or APs of expected size ≥ xρ/B^{O(1)} absorb |𝒱| when 3ν < 1/d.
+- *Moduli sums.* BV/BDH-type sums over |ℐ| ≤ x^{1/2} (modulus, residue) slots have budget ≍ εΨ(x) with
+  ε = L^{−O(1)}. They absorb |𝒱|x^{1/2} iff **5ν < 1/d** (d < 4k/5), with an N^{Ω(1/d)} margin.
+- *Regime.* This holds at the 2/5 design (d ≍ D^{1/5}, ν ≍ D^{−2/5}) and at every fixed d once k > 5d/4.
+- *Not checked.* We did not check every individual input of FM/LD* in its exact indexed form (codex snfsrig_2). The
+  claim is for the class 𝒦 as defined.
+
+**Contrast with Theorem U [consistency check, corrected].** In [U] the rational value G_g(P) of a fixed P has point
+mass ≤ 16/(MV) over g (FIB), and the algebraic value has point mass ≤ 1/((2H + 1)V′^{d−1}) over c (SM1). So deleting
+any K values changes FM's main term by at most K·#𝒫·16/(MV) on the rational side. That is negligible for
+K ≤ N^{μ+ν}ρ_r·L^{−O(1)}, and similarly for K ≤ N^{h+(d−1)ν−o(1)} on the algebraic side. The same statistical inputs
+therefore do suffice there. What matters is quantitative spreading of the queried values, not support alone. This is
+[nest] Prop SI(i)/(ii), applied to the plain smooth set.
+
+**What a proof would need instead.** It would need form-specific, non-relativising information about Norm(a − bα₀) in
+a Kummer field, or about a − b·r^q in base r. Examples are type I/II sums for incomplete norm forms (Heath-Brown,
+Maynard), BBDT's methods, and prescribed-digit methods (Bourgain). §4 and §5 compare the ranges.
 
